The caller's downloaded voice: what is in this folder

Post Time's race caller can read the call in a voice the player downloads once and keeps (js/joe.js).
Nothing in this folder is Post Time's own work but this note and manifest.json. The rest is other
people's, published under the licences below, and the game fetches it only when the player asks for the
voice. manifest.json lists the files that are downloaded, each with its size and SHA-256.
tools/voice-files.mjs made them from the published packages and says exactly how.

1. The voice: Piper's "Joe" (en_US-joe-medium)

   joe/en_US-joe-medium.onnx.part0 to .part3
       The voice's model, cut into four parts of at most 16 MB so that it can be pushed to the repository
       and fetched a part at a time. Put end to end they are the published file, byte for byte:
       63,221,984 bytes, SHA-256 abf4b998e34045cbd00eaf050de2de620f81ded6eb8b85a24b022a1c2b3f08d2.
   joe/en_US-joe-medium.onnx.json    its settings, as published
   joe/MODEL_CARD                    its model card, as published

   From   https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices (en/en_US/joe/medium), at commit
          cf910fb59f1d79ab3b625a63993a108751c6c25d.
   Licence   CC0. The model card says so of its dataset, and that it was fine-tuned from a base that is
          CC0 as well. Piper lists the voice among those "whose entire training chain uses recordings
          whose licenses permit commercial use"
          (https://github.com/OHF-Voice/piper1-gpl/blob/main/docs/COMMERCIAL_USE.md), and asks: "When you
          ship a voice, ship its MODEL_CARD with it." It is here, and in the download.

2. onnxruntime-web 1.22.0, which runs the model

   ort/ort.wasm.min.mjs, ort/ort-wasm-simd-threaded.mjs, ort/ort-wasm-simd-threaded.wasm
       As published (the package's dist/ folder; its plain WebAssembly build).

   From   https://registry.npmjs.org/onnxruntime-web/-/onnxruntime-web-1.22.0.tgz
          (source: https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime, tag v1.22.0)
   Licence   MIT, Copyright (c) Microsoft Corporation. The text is ort/LICENSE.

3. The phonemizer, which turns the words into the sounds the voice says

   phonemize/piper_phonemize.js, phonemize/piper_phonemize.wasm
       As published: piper-phonemize built for the browser, with espeak-ng compiled into it.
       (The package's third file, piper_phonemize.data, is not used: see 4.)

   From   https://registry.npmjs.org/@diffusionstudio/piper-wasm/-/piper-wasm-1.0.0.tgz (5 July 2024),
          source https://github.com/diffusion-studio/piper-wasm. Its README gives the build: Emscripten
          3.1.47, https://github.com/wide-video/piper-phonemize (a fork of
          https://github.com/rhasspy/piper-phonemize) and https://github.com/rhasspy/espeak-ng, each
          cloned at its head in June 2024.
   Licence   The package and piper-phonemize are MIT (Copyright (c) 2023 Michael Hansen; the text is
          phonemize/LICENSE.md). espeak-ng, which is inside piper_phonemize.wasm, is under the GNU General
          Public License, version 3 or later: the text is espeak-ng-data/COPYING, and its source is the
          rhasspy/espeak-ng repository above.

4. espeak-ng's data for English

   espeak-ng-data/en.tar
       A plain tar of espeak-ng's compiled data as Python Piper 1.8.0 ships it (piper/espeak-ng-data in its
       wheel), with two changes:
       - The other languages' dictionaries are left out (every *_dict file but en_dict). The voice speaks
         English, and without them the download is 17 MB smaller.
       - Three bytes of phonindex are changed (at 830: 01 0d 00 20 becomes 02 0d b2 ca). In the source
         that is phsource/phonemes, under "phoneme ;": "ipa NULL" becomes "ipa ʲ". The data told espeak-ng
         not to write the linking sound between a word ending in "ee" and a vowel ("the early lead"). The
         espeak-ng in Python Piper writes it all the same, so Python Piper gives it to the voice; the
         older espeak-ng in piper_phonemize.wasm obeys the data and leaves it out. With the change the
         browser gives the voice the same sounds as Python Piper does: test/joe.test.mjs checks that on
         1,634 lines of race calls.

   From   https://pypi.org/project/piper-tts/1.8.0/
          (piper_tts-1.8.0-cp39-abi3-manylinux_2_17_x86_64.manylinux2014_x86_64.manylinux_2_28_x86_64.whl,
          SHA-256 25b4d3f31ff70c8fa7151908e00aaa5650cbdf16bca8fcf21299f3941b89a7d3). Piper builds it from
          espeak-ng at commit 724808c (CMakeLists.txt at
          https://github.com/OHF-Voice/piper1-gpl/tree/v1.8.0), so the source of the data is
          https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng/tree/724808c (dictsource/ and phsource/).
   Licence   GNU General Public License, version 3 or later (espeak-ng; Piper 1.8.0 is under the same).
          The text is espeak-ng-data/COPYING.

How the game uses them: its own code (js/joe.js, js/joe-worker.js, js/joe-speech.js) loads these files as
they are, in a worker, and passes text in and sound out. None of them is built into the game's code.
